## A blank in the middle

<At levels="simple">

Every model so far has filled in a blank at the end of the text. Put the blank in the middle and
GPT-2 has a problem: it only reads the words before the blank. In *The cat sat on the ___ of the
fridge*, the clue comes after the gap.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Every model so far has filled in a blank at the end of the text. Put the blank in the middle and
GPT-2 has a problem: it only reads the words before the blank. In *The cat sat on the ___ of the
fridge*, the clue comes after the gap, where GPT-2 can't look.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

A different kind of transformer, the <GlossaryPopover term="encoder">encoder</GlossaryPopover>,
reads the whole text at once. The best known is BERT, trained by hiding some words and guessing
them from the words on both sides.<Cite id="devlin2019" /> Here are real guesses from BERT and GPT-2:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A different kind of transformer, the <GlossaryPopover term="encoder">encoder</GlossaryPopover>,
reads the whole text at once. The best known is BERT, trained by hiding 15% of the tokens in its
training text and guessing each one from the words on both sides.<Cite id="devlin2019" /> Here are
real guesses from BERT and GPT-2:

</At>

<FillTheBlank />

For the fridge, GPT-2's best guess is *floor*, a fine way to continue "The cat sat on the", while
BERT's is *top*. GPT-2 sends the shopper for bread to the *hospital*; BERT, which can see "to buy
some bread", says *market*.

<At levels="deep">

The sentence about the dog shows the gap at its widest. In "The ___ wagged its tail and barked",
GPT-2 has only "The" to go on, and its top guess is a new line, at 1.4%. BERT reads the rest and
says *dog*, at 61%. Notice too that BERT's tokenizer split *wagged* into "wa" and "##gged", where
"##" marks a piece that continues the word before it.

</At>

## The same blocks, a different mask

<At levels="simple">

The difference is one setting in attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />). In GPT-2, each token can only look at the
tokens before it. In BERT, every token can look at every other token. Switch between the two:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The difference comes down to one setting in attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />). In GPT-2, a *decoder*, each token can
only look at itself and the tokens before it. In BERT, every token can look at every other token.
Switch between the two:

</At>

<AttentionMask />

Everything else is the recipe you already know: tokens, embeddings, attention, MLPs, and a stack of
blocks. BERT base even has the same size blocks as GPT-2 small: 12 of them, each working on vectors
of 768 numbers.<Cite id="devlin2019" />

<At levels="deep">

### The mask, written down

Attention scores every pair of tokens and turns each row of scores into weights with a softmax. The
only difference between the two models is a mask $M$ added to the scores first:

$$\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d}} + M\right)V$$

For GPT-2, $M_{ij}$ is 0 when token $j$ comes no later than token $i$, and $-\infty$ otherwise, so
later tokens get a weight of exactly 0. For BERT, every $M_{ij}$ is 0. Over the 10 tokens of the
fridge sentence, GPT-2's mask allows 55 of the 100 pairs and BERT's all 100.

BERT pays for that in training. Only the 15% of tokens it hides give it something to predict, while
GPT-2 learns from every token. And of the hidden tokens, BERT swapped 80% for a `[MASK]` token, 10%
for a random token, and left 10% unchanged, so that it couldn't count on seeing `[MASK]`, which
never appears in real text.<Cite id="devlin2019" />

</At>

## Good at understanding, not at writing

<At levels="simple">

The price of looking both ways is that an encoder needs the whole text before it starts, so it
can't write one token at a time. Encoders are used for *understanding* instead: search, spam
filters, and embeddings like the map in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The price of looking both ways is that an encoder needs the whole text before it starts. It can't
write a story one token at a time, because the tokens after the one it's writing don't exist yet.
So encoders are used for *understanding*: search, sorting email into spam or not, and embeddings.
The embedding model behind the map in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" /> is a small encoder of the same design as BERT.

</At>

The original transformer had both halves: an encoder to read a sentence in one language and a
decoder to write it in another.<Cite id="vaswani2017" /> Many systems still pair them, like an
encoder that finds the relevant documents (<ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />) and a decoder that writes the answer.

<RecapCard>

- GPT-2 is a decoder: each token sees only earlier tokens, which is what lets it write left to
  right.
- An encoder like BERT lets every token see every other one, so it can use the words on both sides
  of a blank.
- Encoders are used for understanding: search, classification, and embeddings.

<At levels="deep">

- Attention adds a mask before the softmax: $-\infty$ for later tokens in GPT-2, nothing in BERT.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** every one of GPT-2's weights works on every token. Could a model be huge but use
only part of itself for each token?
